**ADHD (attention deficit hyperactivity disorder)** is a condition that affects how the brain manages **attention, activity and impulses**. It is a **neurodevelopmental** condition, which means it starts in childhood and affects how the brain develops. It is not caused by bad parenting or lack of effort.

## Signs of ADHD

ADHD looks different in everyone. Signs are usually grouped into two types, and many people have both.

**Inattention:**

- being easily distracted and finding it hard to focus
- forgetting things and losing things
- starting tasks but not finishing them
- finding organising, planning and time management hard

**Hyperactivity and impulsiveness:**

- restlessness and fidgeting
- talking a lot or interrupting
- acting without thinking
- finding it hard to wait

In adults, hyperactivity often shows as **inner restlessness**. Many adults, especially women, have mainly inattentive symptoms, which are easier to miss.

## Getting assessed

- **Children:** talk to your child's GP or school, who can refer to a specialist service.
- **Adults:** see your GP, who can refer you to an adult ADHD service. A specialist, usually a psychiatrist, makes the diagnosis.

Waiting lists are long in many areas. In **England**, you can use **Right to Choose** to choose another NHS-funded provider, which may have a shorter wait. Ask your GP.

## Conditions that often come with ADHD

ADHD often occurs with **anxiety, depression, autism, dyslexia, dyspraxia** and **sleep problems**.

## Treatment

Treatment can include medicine, support and practical strategies. See [ADHD treatment](/conditions/mental-health/adhd/treatment/).
